# mdsvex — `.svx`

This page **is** a `.svx` file. mdsvex compiles it to a Svelte component, and
`@fractalpop/mdsvex` highlights every fenced code block at build time. Nothing
here runs client-side — the highlighting is baked into the HTML.

The setup is one option in `svelte.config.js`:

```ts
import { fractalpopHighlighter } from '@fractalpop/mdsvex'

mdsvex({ highlight: { highlighter: fractalpopHighlighter } })
```

## Indented Sass

Fence meta `{2,3}` highlights those lines:

```sass {2,3}
$brand: hsl(212, 90%, 55%)
.card
  color: $brand
  &:hover
    background: darken($brand, 8%) !important
```

## TypeScript

Regex literals and template interpolation are handled:

```ts
type User = { name: string }
const greet = (u: User) => `Hi ${u.name}`
const slug = /^[a-z]+$/i
```

## A Svelte component

The `.svelte` composite colours `<script>`, markup, and `<style lang="sass">`:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  let count = $state(0)
</script>

<button onclick={() => count++}>Count: {count}</button>

<style lang="sass">
  button
    color: $brand
</style>
```

Because mdsvex is a Svelte preprocessor, you can mix real components in too —
this counter below the fences is live Svelte, not highlighted code.

<script>
  import '$lib/styles/fractalpop-demo.sass';
  let clicks = $state(0)
</script>

<button class="live-btn" onclick={() => clicks++}>Clicked {clicks} times</button>
